-- Option (b), decided in TEMPORAL-RULE-VERSION-DESIGN.md §6 Q1:
-- when NO rule-version is pinned, `RULES EFFECTIVE DATE` falls back to the
-- fact/valid-time axis (tcValidTime) before falling back to "today". So
-- law-time tracks fact-time by default — the presumption against
-- retroactivity: apply the law in force at the time of the facts. This is
-- also consistent with the interval builtins, which stamp valid-time and
-- rule-effective-time together per stepped day.
--
-- Harness clock: JL4_FIXED_NOW = 2025-01-31 (post-2024 regime).

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

TIMEZONE IS "Etc/UTC"

-- GST rose from 7 to 9 effective 2024-01-01.
`GST rate` MEANS
  IF DATE_SERIAL `RULES EFFECTIVE DATE` AT LEAST DATE_SERIAL (Date 1 1 2024)
  THEN 9
  ELSE 7

-- expected: 7 — facts pinned to 2019, no rule-version: law-time TRACKS
-- fact-time, so the OLD regime applies. (Under option (a) this would be 9.)
#EVAL `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 6 2019) `GST rate`

-- expected: 9 — facts pinned to 2024: NEW regime. (Same shared thunk as
-- above; the crValidTime fingerprint invalidates the 2019-regime cache.)
#EVAL `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 7 2024) `GST rate`

-- expected: 7 — the rule-version axis WINS over fact-time: even with facts
-- in 2024, an explicit EVAL UNDER RULES EFFECTIVE AT forces the 2019 law.
-- Decoupling fact-time from law-time is the explicit (inner) act.
#EVAL `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 7 2024) (`EVAL UNDER RULES EFFECTIVE AT` (Date 1 6 2019) `GST rate`)

-- expected: 9 — both axes unset: falls back to "today" (2025-01-31), new regime
#EVAL `GST rate`
